    {
      "phrase": "no value repeated",
      "meaning": "At most one index can hold the target, so you can stop as soon as the middle matches. With repeats, you'd have to hunt for the first or last copy.",
      "pointsTo": "binary_search"
    }
  ],
  "constraintReading": "len(nums) is at most 10⁴, so even a full scan would finish quickly: the size alone allows O(n). The O(log n) time target is what rules out O(n) and reading every number. Halving 10⁴ numbers takes at most 14 checks.",
  "hints": {
    "clarify": "Do you return the number or its index, and what if `target` isn't in `nums`? Try [2, 5, 9, 12, 20] by hand: a target of 12 gives index 3, and a target of 10 gives -1, because 10 would sit between 9 and 12 but isn't there.",
    "approach": "Binary search. Because `nums` is sorted, the middle value tells you which half can still hold `target`: if it's too small, everything to its left is too small as well. Keep `lo` and `hi` at the two ends of the part that's still possible, check the middle, and move one end past it. Each check halves what's left, so 10⁴ numbers take at most 14 checks.",
    "whyNot": "Reading `nums` from left to right works, but it's O(n) time, and the problem asks for O(log n). A scan ignores the sorted order, which is what lets you skip numbers without reading them. A dict from value to index doesn't help either: building it reads every number, so it costs O(n) time and O(n) space before the first lookup.",
    "slots": {
      "setup": "`lo` at 0 and `hi` at the last index: at the start, `target` could be anywhere in `nums`.",
      "loop": "Keep going while `lo` is at most `hi`, so at least one index is left to check. Each round, set `mid` halfway between them, rounding down with integer division.",
      "update": "The value at `mid` isn't the target, so drop the half that can't hold it. If it's smaller than `target`, move `lo` to the index just after `mid`; if it's larger, move `hi` to the index just before `mid`.",
      "record": "Compare the value at `mid` with `target`. If they're equal, you've found it: return `mid`.",
      "return": "If the loop ends, `lo` has passed `hi` and no index is left, so `target` isn't in `nums`: return -1."
    }
  },
  "solution": {
    "code": "class Solution:\n    def search(self, nums: list[int], target: int) -> int:\n        lo, hi = 0, len(nums) - 1\n        while lo <= hi:\n            mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n            if nums[mid] == target:  # viz:check\n                return mid  # viz:found\n            if nums[mid] < target:\n                lo = mid + 1  # viz:go_right\n            else:\n                hi = mid - 1  # viz:go_left\n        return -1  # viz:missing\n",
    "explanation": "Each round either returns or halves the part of `nums` that can still hold `target`, so n numbers need at most log₂ n + 1 rounds: O(log n) time. Only three integers are stored, `lo`, `hi` and `mid`, so the space is O(1).",
    "toolkit": ["divmod"]
  },
